.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1143 AM PDT Sun Sep 6 2026
(This afternoon through Monday)

Hazardous conditions at Pacific Coast beaches continues to be the
weather headline through Monday. Long period southerly swell from
distant Hurricane Marie will result in an increased risk for sneaker
waves and strong rip currents with breaking waves up to 10 feet.
Stay off the beach and remain out of the water to mitigate your
risk. Minor coastal flooding will be possible during high tide this
evening for bayshore locations along the San Pablo Bay in Marin
County. Today will be the last day of well below normal temperatures
before the warming and drying trend kicks off Monday. The marine
layer continues to struggle to come back in full force with low
confidence in it doing so through Monday and even into the long term
forecast.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1143 AM PDT Sun Sep 6 2026
(Monday night through next Saturday)

Anomalously high heights from upper-level ridging across the Desert
Southwest will nose in from the east on Tuesday, peaking Wednesday
into Thursday. The forecast has trended significantly warmer and
drier. The reason being an upper-level shortwave trough will dig
well of the West Coast and join forces with Post-Tropical Cyclone
Marie and become a cutoff low. The overall weakness of the low and
its independence from the jet stream will give the ridge precedence
over the forecast as its allowed to join forces with ridging over
the Northeastern Pacific Ocean. A weaker than normal sea breeze
circulation will mean that even the coast will be impacted due to
limited onshore flow and thus marine layer formation. There is now
widespread moderate to areas of major HeatRisk, meaning that at this
point a Heat Advisory would be needed for most of the area Wednesday
and Thursday. Temperatures will be 10 to 15 degrees above normal and
likely feel worse due to us coming off the heels of well below
normal temperatures. To put this upcoming event into context, 850 mb
temperatures and 500 mb heights will be between the 90th percentile
and record values. Needless to say, rain chances from remnant
tropical moisture have been wiped out. An approaching upper-level
trough will tamp down the ridge Friday into next weekend, cooling
temperatures to near normal and increasing onshore flow.

